  "slug": "pangenome-graphs-bd3e",
  "summary": "Recent preprints move haplotype, ancestry and mtDNA analyses away from linear references and variant calls toward graph and k-mer methods.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is departure from linear-reference or variant-call inputs in genome analysis. GRASER phases graph-aligned short reads directly against individual haplotype paths, using unique graph edges to avoid linear-reference bias. The gAIRR Suite v2 study applies population-scale pangenome analysis to 232 individuals, reporting 531 novel TR germline alleles and 99.985% recall. KORUS infers ancestry from k-mer Bloom filter representations without variant calls, supporting classification, admixture fractions and population structure. Other items are less directly aligned with this thread: a public haploblock-based hash dataset for 2,548 1000 Genomes individuals, and MitoArc, a Nextflow workflow for short-read mtDNA analysis with circular-coordinate processing and NUMT-aware evaluation on synthetic truth data. What is new is the use of graph and k-mer structures for haplotype, germline and ancestry inference at population scale.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
